    "sourceName": "IELTS-Blog",
    "wordCount": 750,
    "timeSpent": 40,
    "isAIGenerated": false,
    "content": "The benefits of giving or not giving homework to children are often debated. Some people feel that children have enough work to do during one day whilst at school, whereas others feel that the more work set, the better.\n\nIt is true that schools today place more and more demands and pressure on children. The competition to do well, to enter a good university and to get a good job is significant. This has led to the ideology that children should do as much schoolwork as possible in order to help their intellectual development. The argument, however, that more is better is not always valid. There comes a point, especially for a child, when more knowledge cannot be absorbed in a day. When a child has possibly 7 or 8 classes a day on different subjects, he or she is processing a lot of information already. To give more work to be done at home, therefore, would be pointless and even counterproductive. In addition to this, although it is important to be educated, childhood should also be a time when there is an opportunity to just play and enjoy unstructured time. Homework can significantly lessen the possibilities of doing this.\n\nOn the other hand, it can be argued that there is a place for homework. If study at school is not overdone, the opportunity to look at work again and extend ideas can be an invaluable exercise. It’s easy to forget one thing that has been taught once, but if that concept is revisited a second time, the brain has time to reprocess and likely better understand and remember.\n\nIt seems to me that there is a place for homework for today’s children. What must be remembered, though, is that homework needs to be limited to age-appropriate parameters, so that a balance can be struck between study and free time and relaxation.\n\nGo here for more IELTS Band 9 Essays\n\nYou’re welcome, I am glad our essays are useful!\n\nIts an amazig sample answer and help me a lot to gain ideas about this topic and improve my wroting skills also\n\nKeep up the good work, Harshdeep! It’s also a good idea to write down some vocabulary from the model answer and then try and use it in your own essay on this topic. Make sure you know how to use the new words though!\n\nAn influential contemporary trend involves a growing number of individual expressing their opinion the subject of beliving that homework should not be given to school chidren while it is thrue that every topic has its dvantages and disadvantagea , i belive that beliving that homework should not be given to school childern. Also , on the one hand , critics may also point out some drawbacks, they might say that The childrens boring to the school lesson, also children forget provious studies , some children adictive to the online games. On the other hand , there are several arguments. In my favour of my stance of the stongest reasons is that children not bocome the streea mind they spend time very relax.This isthe most compelling arguments as it leads to various advantages. Moveover another noteworthy benefit is that the children fouces to extra activitiea in the eveining.exsample: The children come to the home do panting , arts , dancing , music, ctivities it deveoped extra qualifications.which further highlights its significant Further , they can enjoy family members it deveolped family relationshpis also, children facous to the future plans . Also a recent report by prestigous universoty proves validity of the statment with all the avilable logical evidence. In conclution , there are several argumnts the both side of the benefit of beliving that home work should not be given to school childeren.out weigh the some drawbacks.considering the positivw impact on individuals and soceity it is essential to facous on maxmising these advantagea while addresing any concerns.\n\nYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *\n\nName *\n\nEmail *\n\nWebsite\n\nComment *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment.\n\nSimone Braverman is the founder of IELTS-Blog.com and author of several popular IELTS preparation books, including Ace the IELTS, Target Band 7, the High Scorer's Choice practice test series, and IELTS Success Formula. Since 2005 her work has helped 100,000's of students worldwide achieve their target IELTS scores and live their dream lives. When Simone isn't working on her next IELTS book, video lesson, or coaching, she enjoys playing the guitar or rollerblading.",
